{
  "sn35.241:0.1": "Linked Discourses 35.241 ",
  "sn35.241:0.2": "19. The Simile of the Vipers ",
  "sn35.241:0.3": "The Simile of the Tree Trunk (1st) ",
  "sn35.241:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Kosambī on the bank of the Ganges River. ",
  "sn35.241:1.2": "Seeing a large tree trunk being carried along by the current, ",
  "sn35.241:1.3": "he addressed the mendicants, ",
  "sn35.241:1.4": "“Mendicants, do you see that large tree trunk being carried along by the current of the Ganges River?” ",
  "sn35.241:1.5": "“Yes, sir.” ",
  "sn35.241:1.6": "“Mendicants, assume that that tree trunk doesn’t drift toward the near shore or the far shore, or sink in the middle, or get stranded on high ground. And assume that it doesn’t get taken by humans or non-humans or caught up in a whirlpool, and that it doesn’t rot away. ",
  "sn35.241:1.7": "In that case, that tree trunk will slant, slope, and incline toward the ocean. ",
  "sn35.241:1.8": "Why is that? ",
  "sn35.241:1.9": "Because the current of the Ganges River slants, slopes, and inclines toward the ocean. ",
  "sn35.241:2.1": "In the same way, assume that you don’t drift toward the near shore or the far shore, or sink in the middle, or get stranded on high ground. And assume that you don’t get taken by humans or non-humans or caught up in a whirlpool, and that you don’t rot away. ",
  "sn35.241:2.2": "",
  "sn35.241:2.3": "In that case, you will slant, slope, and incline toward extinguishment. ",
